Specification Books

Description The book: one file, one chart of accounts, one set of transactions.
# There are two of them here, a business one and a personal one, and
# nothing joins them.
Details
# Every other specification in this folder is written about one book and
# says so by saying nothing: "the chart of accounts" means the chart of
# accounts of the book being worked in. This file is what makes that
# precise, now that there are two.
#
# Two books rather than one set of accounts with a business branch is the
# decision. A sole proprietor files a business return and a personal one,
# and the figures on each must come from somewhere that cannot
# accidentally include the other. Separate files give that by
# construction: there is no query that can reach across them, so no
# report can be wrong in that particular way.

Entity Book
Description One book, which is one file on disk.
| Attribute        | Type        | Default    | Notes                           |
| Name             | Text        |            | What the user calls it          |
| FilePath         | Text        |            | Where it is stored              |
| FiscalYearStart  | Integer     | 1          | Month; see the rule in Reports  |
| FirstDate        | Date        | 2005-01-01 | Of the earliest transaction     |
| LastDate         | Date        | 2026-12-31 | Of the latest                   |

Collection Books
Description The books the program knows about. Not a container: each is its own
# file, and this is the list of them that the user has opened.
| DataType | Minimum | Maximum | Notes                        |
| Book     | 0       | 1000    | FilePath is unique            |

Background The two books
Given books are : Books
| Name     | FilePath              | FiscalYearStart | FirstDate  | LastDate   |
| Business | books/business.sadb   | 1               | 2005-06-17 | 2026-01-31 |
| Personal | books/personal.sadb   | 1               | 2017-01-02 | 2025-12-09 |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What belongs to a book, and what does not
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule Everything a book needs is in the book
Description A book is self-contained. Anything the program needs in order to
# work on a book is stored in that book's own file, even where two
# books would hold the same thing.
Details An earlier reading of this had the bank profiles shared, on the
# grounds that the same bank sends the same CSV layout to a business
# account and a personal one, so matching its headings is a fact about
# the bank rather than about either book. That is true and it is not the
# point. Setting a profile up twice is a minute's work; a book that
# cannot be opened, copied or carried to another machine on its own is
# a permanent cost. Separation is worth more than the minute.
#
# So almost nothing is stored outside a book. The window's own state --
# its size, which columns are where -- is the program's settings rather
# than anybody's data, and one thing more is kept beside the books.
#
# That one thing is the QIF profile, and it is worth saying why it is not
# per book when the CSV profile is. A CSV profile describes a bank, and
# the same bank writes the same layout to a business account and a
# personal one, so duplicating it costs a minute and keeps the books
# apart. A QIF profile describes how Quicken itself writes a file -- the
# date order, whether the decimal mark is a comma, what to do with trade
# history -- and that is one answer for the machine, not one per book.
# Importing the same export a second time should not ask again, and
# importing from a second Quicken book should not ask at all.
Examples: BookScope
| Thing                        | Scope    | Why                                       |
| Accounts                     | PerBook  | A fact about money                        |
| Transactions and splits      | PerBook  |                                           |
| Categories                   | PerBook  | The business chart is not the personal one |
| Payees                       | PerBook  | Derived from the transactions of the book  |
| Payee rules                  | PerBook  | A business payee takes a business category |
| Remembered splits            | PerBook  |                                           |
| Bank numbers on an account   | PerBook  | They are part of the account, which is per book |
| Reconciliations              | PerBook  |                                           |
| Fiscal year start            | PerBook  | A business year need not be a personal one |
| CSV bank profiles            | PerBook  | Duplicated rather than shared; see above  |
| CSV header aliases           | PerBook  | Likewise                                  |
| The QIF profile              | Machine  | How Quicken writes, not what a book holds |
| Window size and column order | Settings | The program's, not a book's               |

Attributes BookScope
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Thing     | String |         |
| Scope     | String |         |
| Why       | String | none    |

Scenario A category in one book is not in the other
Description The business book has categories the personal one has no use for,
# and the pickers in each offer only their own.
Given books are as previous
Given the chart of accounts of Business is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path                     | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses                 | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Consulting Fees | Expense | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries       | Expense | false       |
Given the chart of accounts of Personal is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Expenses           | Expense | true        |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
When category picker opened in Personal
Then category picker offers : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path               |
| Expenses:Groceries |
# Expenses:Consulting Fees is absent: it is not in this book at all, which is
# different from being hidden.

Scenario A payee rule made in one book does not act in the other
Description The rules are learned from categorising, and a category learned in
# the business book names a business category. Applying it in the
# personal book would either fail or create that category there.
