| NetWorth    | 4000.00 |

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where a book is kept
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule A book is one SQLite database in one known folder
Description Every book is a single file with a .sadb name, and they all live in
# one folder that the program knows without being told. There is no
# "where would you like to save this": a book is named, and the name
# decides the file.
Details The folder is ProgramData\SimpleAccount. It is machine-wide rather than
# per-user, so the books on a machine are the books, whoever is signed
# in. As many of them as the user makes can live there side by side.
#
# The program makes the folder itself, the first time a book is created.
# That needs no installer and no elevation: a process running as an
# ordinary user may create a subfolder of ProgramData and owns what it
# puts there.
#
# Nothing is ever created implicitly. A database is initialised when the
# user asks for a new book and at no other time, so opening a book that
# is not there is refused rather than quietly made -- a mistyped name
# must not look like a book that lost all its transactions.
Examples: BookFile
| Action                        | Name     | Effect                                |
| New book                      | Business | Created, initialised, and opened      |
| New book, the second time     | Business | Refused; that book already exists     |
| Open book                     | Business | Opened                                |
| Open book                     | Typo     | Refused; there is no book of that name |
| List books                    |          | Every .sadb file in the folder        |

Attributes BookFile
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Action    | String |         |
| Name      | Text   | none    |
| Effect    | String |         |

BusinessRule One table per collection, one row per entity
Description The schema is not designed separately from this specification: it is
# read off it. Every Entity declared anywhere in these files becomes a
# row, and the Collection it belongs to becomes the table. Its
# attributes become the columns, with the same names and the same
# defaults.
Details So there is nothing to keep in step by hand. Adding an attribute to an
# Entity adds a column; declaring a new Entity that belongs in a book
# adds a table. The check that the two have not drifted is mechanical:
# every Entity in these files appears below, in one of the three
# groups, and a reviewer can see at a glance that none was forgotten.
#
# Which group an Entity is in follows from the rule above. Nearly
# everything is in the book's own file. One thing sits beside the books,
# because it describes Quicken rather than any book of ours. And what is
# only ever being looked at is stored nowhere: a statement being
# imported, a Quicken report being compared against. Those belong to the
# act of importing rather than to a book.
#
# Holdings are the exception and are marked "not yet" rather than left
# out: investments are carried as balances only in this round, so there
# is nothing to store per security. The row stays in the table above so
# that the count of entities still matches and nobody has to wonder
# whether it was forgotten.
Examples: EntityStorage
| Entity            | Collection          | Table                 | Where       |
| Account           | ChartOfAccounts     | accounts              | The book    |
| Transaction       |                     | transactions          | The book    |
| TransactionSplit  | TransactionSplits   | transaction_splits    | The book    |
| ImportId          | ImportIds           | transaction_import_ids | The book   |
| Holding           | Holdings            |                       | Not yet     |
| PayeeRule         | PayeeRules          | payee_rules           | The book    |
| PayeeSplitRule    |                     | payee_split_rules     | The book    |
| TransferMatchRule | TransferMatchRules  | transfer_match_rules  | The book    |
| Book              | Books               |                       | The folder  |
| CsvProfile        |                     | csv_profiles          | The book    |
| HeaderMatch       | HeaderMatches       | csv_header_matches    | The book    |
| QifProfile        |                     | qif_profiles          | Beside      |
| OfxStatement      |                     |                       | Nowhere     |
| OfxTransaction    | OfxTransactions     |                       | Nowhere     |
| QuickenReport     |                     |                       | Nowhere     |

Attributes EntityStorage
| Attribute  | Type   | Default |
| Entity     | String |         |
| Collection | String | none    |
| Table      | String | none    |
| Where      | String |         |

BusinessRule What a book's file holds besides its entities
Description Two things that are not entities and are needed anyway: which
# version the schema is, and the book's own name and fiscal year.
Details The version is read before anything else. A file written by a later
# version of the program is refused and left untouched, because the
# alternative is a program guessing at columns it does not know and
# writing the book back in a shape that loses them. Being told to
# upgrade is a better morning than finding a silently emptied register.
#
# The Book entity is the odd one out: there is no table of books inside a
# book, and no table of books anywhere. The folder is the collection --
# one file per book -- and a book is chosen from the file names in it
# with the extension taken off, so the list is made by looking rather
# than maintained.
#
# What a book stores about itself is a single row in a table of its own.
# A text file of the same name beside the database would do as well, and
# the reason to prefer the row is that there is then one file per book:
# copying it, backing it up or moving it to another machine cannot take
# half of a book, and there is no second file to fall out of step.
#
# Amounts are stored as whole cents in an integer column. A binary
# floating-point column cannot hold 0.10, and a book that cannot add up
# is the one thing this program may not be.
#
# The settings a book carries go in that same row rather than in a table
# of name and value pairs. There are few of them, each means something
# different, and a row of named columns can be read without a join and
# cannot hold a setting nobody recognises. See the check-number window in
# ImportOfx for the first one that is not simply descriptive.
Examples: BookInfrastructure
| Table  | Columns                        | Notes                              |
| schema | version                        | One row; read before anything else |
| book   | name, fiscal_year_start, check_window_days, last_id_used | One row; the Book entity's own data, and the id counter |

Attributes BookInfrastructure
| Attribute | Type   | Default |
| Table     | String |         |
| Columns   | String |         |
| Notes     | String | none    |

BusinessRule How an attribute becomes a column
Description Mechanically, by its type, so that no column is a judgement call.
Examples: ColumnType
| AttributeType  | Column  | Notes                                        |
| Money          | INTEGER | Whole cents; never a floating-point column   |
| Date           | TEXT    | ISO, so it sorts and compares as text        |
| Integer        | INTEGER |                                              |
| Boolean        | INTEGER | 0 or 1                                       |
| Text           | TEXT    |                                              |
| AccountPath    | TEXT    | The path as a person reads it                |
| AccountId      | INTEGER | The identity a stored reference names         |
| TransactionRef | TEXT    |                                              |
| An enumeration | TEXT    | The value's own name, as this file writes it |

Attributes ColumnType
| Attribute     | Type   | Default |
| AttributeType | String |         |
| Column        | String |         |
| Notes         | String | none    |

$TBD
BusinessRule The QIF profiles live in one file beside the books, under a name
Description Named, so there can be more than one: an export made years ago may
# have a different date order from one made today, and being able to
# keep both beats answering the question again from memory.
Details The file is settings.sadata in the same folder, and the extension is
# what keeps it out of the way: a book is a .sadb file, so the list of
# books does not offer this one and a stray file is never mistaken for a
# book. It carries its own schema version, read first, for the same
# reason a book does.
#
# It is made on demand, like the folder. A machine that has never
# imported a QIF file has no such file and needs none.
Examples: BesideTheBooks
| File            | Holds                     | InBookList |
| Business.sadb   | A book                    | Yes        |
| Personal.sadb   | A book                    | Yes        |
| settings.sadata | The QIF profiles, by name | No         |
| notes.txt       | Nothing of ours           | No         |

Attributes BesideTheBooks
| Attribute  | Type   | Default |
| File       | Text   |         |
| Holds      | String |         |
| InBookList | String |         |

