Scenario A book written and opened again is the same book
Description The round trip is the whole of what storage has to do, so it is
# stated as one scenario over a book with something in it: a chart,
# a transaction naming both its accounts, its memo, and its cleared
# status.
Given a new book named Business
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Memo   | Cleared |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00 | Apples | Cleared |
When the book is saved and opened again
Then the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type    | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense | false       |
Then transaction is : Transaction Vertical CompareOnly
| Ref           | T1                 |
| SourceAccount | Assets:Checking    |
| Destination   | Expenses:Groceries |
| Amount        | -45.00             |
| Memo          | Apples             |
| Cleared       | Cleared            |
# The memo is the transaction's one memo, so it reads the same from either
# register regardless of which side it was typed against, and the cleared
# marker is the one account's own -- the account this entry lives in.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a save writes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule A save leaves the file holding exactly what the book holds
Description A save is under no obligation to write everything, and it is under
# an obligation to leave nothing behind. What is specified is the
# state of the file afterwards: every change the book has taken is in
# it, every row the book no longer has is gone from it, and a row
# nobody touched reads back as it was.
Details The first implementation wrote everything -- every table emptied and
# every row inserted again, twenty years of history for one new
# transaction. That is correct, and it is most of why entering a
# transaction takes as long as it does.
#
# The rule is stated about the file afterwards rather than about the
# rows written, because the second would be a specification of the
# mechanism. Nobody reading a register can tell an UPDATE from a
# DELETE followed by an INSERT. They can tell a stale row.
#
# A save is all or nothing. If it cannot be completed -- the disk is
# full, another process holds the file -- what is on disk afterwards is
# the book as it was before the save, not half of each. A half-written
# save is the worst outcome available here: a register would read back
# with one side of a transfer missing, and nothing would say so.
#
# Deletion is what an incremental save gets wrong, so it is below
# twice: a transaction deleted, and a split with one line fewer than it
# had. The row nobody asked about is the same rule read the other way,
# and it is in the editing scenario -- the transaction beside the
# edited one must read back untouched.
Examples: SaveEffect
| Change                | Afterwards                                      |
| A transaction added   | Its row is there, and no other row has altered  |
| A transaction edited  | Its row holds the new values                    |
| A transaction deleted | No row for it, and none for its split lines     |
| A split line removed  | No row for that line; the other lines as before |
| An account renamed    | Its row holds the new path                      |
| A payee rule added    | Its row is there, and the earlier rules untouched |
| Nothing changed       | The file as it was                              |
| The save fails        | The file as it was before the save              |

Attributes SaveEffect
| Attribute  | Type   | Default |
| Change     | String |         |
| Afterwards | String |         |

Scenario A transaction edited after a save reads back edited
Description And the transaction beside it, which nobody touched, reads back as
# it was. Both halves matter: the edit has to reach the file, and
# reaching it must not disturb anything else.
Given a new book named Business
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Memo   | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00 | Apples | Cleared   |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-16 | Lowes         | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00 | Nails  | Uncleared |
When the book is saved
