| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Memo             | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -52.00 | Apples and pears | Cleared   |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-16 | Lowes         | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00 | Nails            | Uncleared |
When the book is saved and opened again
Then transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Memo             | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -52.00 | Apples and pears | Cleared   |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-16 | Lowes         | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00 | Nails            | Uncleared |
# Two saves of the same book, with one row different between them. The second
# save is the one under test; the first is what gives it something to be
# incremental against.

Scenario A transaction deleted after a save is gone from the file
Description Along with its split lines. A transaction is deleted from the
# register and the lines beneath it are nobody's any more: left in
# the file, they would be read back as a split of a transaction
# that no longer exists.
Given a new book named Business
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
| Expenses:Household | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Memo   | Cleared   |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Split              | -45.00 | none   | Cleared   |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-16 | Lowes         | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00 | Nails  | Uncleared |
Given split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination        | Amount | Memo   |
| Expenses:Groceries | 30.00  | Apples |
| Expenses:Household | 15.00  | Soap   |
When the book is saved
When transactions become : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee | Destination        | Amount | Memo  | Cleared   |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-16 | Lowes | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00 | Nails | Uncleared |
When the book is saved and opened again
Then transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee | Destination        | Amount | Memo  | Cleared   |
| T2  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-16 | Lowes | Expenses:Groceries | -12.00 | Nails | Uncleared |
Then split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination | Amount | Memo |
# No rows: T1's two lines went with it.

Scenario A split line removed after a save is not read back
Description The split had two lines and now has one. The line that went has to
# go from the file as well, because a save that wrote the lines it
# has and left the rest would read the split back as seventy-five.
Given a new book named Business
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
| Expenses:Household | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination | Amount | Memo | Cleared |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Split       | -45.00 | none | Cleared |
Given split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination        | Amount | Memo   |
| Expenses:Groceries | 30.00  | Apples |
| Expenses:Household | 15.00  | Soap   |
When the book is saved
When split lines become : TransactionSplit
| Destination        | Amount | Memo   |
| Expenses:Groceries | 45.00  | Apples |
When the book is saved and opened again
Then split lines are : TransactionSplit
| Destination        | Amount | Memo   |
| Expenses:Groceries | 45.00  | Apples |

Scenario A second save with nothing changed leaves the book as it was
Description The common case while somebody is reading rather than typing. A save
# with nothing to write is still a save: it may not empty anything,
# and what was there is read back whole.
Given a new book named Business
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
Given transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Memo   | Cleared |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00 | Apples | Cleared |
When the book is saved
When the book is saved and opened again
Then the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
Then transactions are : Transaction
| Ref | SourceAccount   | Date       | Payee         | Destination        | Amount | Memo   | Cleared |
| T1  | Assets:Checking | 2026-01-15 | Harris Teeter | Expenses:Groceries | -45.00 | Apples | Cleared |

Scenario An account renamed after a save reads back renamed
Description The account kept its identity and changed its name, so the file has
# to hold the new path against the same row rather than a second row
# under the new name. The account renamed here is one no transaction
# names, so what is under test is the chart alone.
Given a new book named Business
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Assets:Savings     | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
When the book is saved
When account renamed : AccountRename Vertical
| Path    | Assets:Savings |
| NewName | Rainy Day      |
When the book is saved and opened again
Then the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Assets:Rainy Day   | Checking | false       |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
# Three accounts afterwards, not four. A rename that wrote a row and left the
# old one would show the account twice in every picker in the program.

Scenario A payee rule added after a save leaves the earlier rules alone
Description Rules are learned one at a time, as categories are chosen, so this
# is the shape of nearly every save the rules table ever sees.
Given a new book named Business
Given payee rules of Business are : PayeeRules
| Pattern | MatchType | Payee  |
| STAPLES | Contains  | Office |
When the book is saved
When payee rules become : PayeeRules
| Pattern       | MatchType | Payee         |
| STAPLES       | Contains  | Office        |
| HARRIS TEETER | Contains  | Harris Teeter |
When the book is saved and opened again
Then payee rules in force in Business are : PayeeRules
| Pattern       | MatchType | Payee         |
| STAPLES       | Contains  | Office        |
| HARRIS TEETER | Contains  | Harris Teeter |

Scenario Opening a book that is not there is refused
Description And says so, rather than making one. A mistyped name is the likely
# cause, and an empty book would look like a catastrophe.
When a book is opened named : BookName Vertical
| Name | Typo |
Then the book is refused saying
"""
There is no book named Typo
"""

Scenario Making a book that already exists is refused
Description The existing one is not touched. Overwriting a book of twenty years
# of history because a name was reused is not a mistake worth making
# available.
Given a new book named Business
When a new book is made named : BookName Vertical
| Name | Business |
Then the book is refused saying
"""
A book named Business already exists
"""

Scenario A book from a later version of the program is refused
Description Rather than opened and written back in a shape that drops whatever
# the later version added.
Given a book whose schema version is 99
When a book is opened named : BookName Vertical
| Name | Business |
Then the book is refused saying
"""
Business was written by a later version of SimpleAccount
"""

Scenario The books in the folder are listed by name
Description What the open dialog offers. The name is the file name without its
# extension, because the extension is not the user's business.
Given the books folder holds : BookFileList
| FileName       |
| Business.sadb  |
| Personal.sadb  |
| notes.txt      |
When the books are listed
Then the book names are : BookNameRow
| Name     |
| Business |
| Personal |
# notes.txt is not a book and is not offered as one.

Attributes BookFileList
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| FileName  | Text |         |

Attributes BookNameRow
| Attribute | Type | Default |
| Name      | Text |         |

