$TBD
Scenario Each QIF file migrates into its own book
Description The two exports in testdata become the two books, and the
# migration is run twice. Neither run can see the other, so a
# transfer whose other half is in the other file is unpaired rather
# than matched across the two. See ImportQif.
Given no books exist
When qif file migrated into a new book : QifMigration
| SourceFile      | BookName |
| pughkilleen.QIF | Business |
| kenpugh.QIF     | Personal |
Then books are : Books CompareOnly
| Name     | FirstDate  | LastDate   |
| Business | 2005-06-17 | 2026-01-31 |
| Personal | 2017-01-02 | 2025-12-09 |

Attributes QifMigration
| Attribute  | Type | Default |
| SourceFile | Text |         |
| BookName   | Text |         |

$TBD
Scenario A bracketed account naming the other file is created in this book
Description The business export may name an account that lives in the personal
# one. The migration cannot reach across, so it creates the account
# in the book it is building and reports it, as it does for any
# bracketed name it has not seen. Whether the two should be joined
# afterwards is a judgement, and it is left to the user.
Given no books exist
Given the current book is : CurrentBook Vertical
| Name | Business |
Given the file contains
"""
!Type:Bank
D4/ 1'25
T-4000.00
POwner draw
L[Personal Checking]
^
"""
Given import target is : ImportTarget Vertical
| AccountPath | Assets:Checking |
When qif file imported
Then accounts created are : ChartOfAccounts CompareOnly
| Path                     |
| Assets:Checking          |
| Assets:Personal Checking |
Then migration notes are : MigrationNote
| Kind           | Detail                                                        |
| AccountGuessed | "[Personal Checking]" was not in the account list; made Assets:Personal Checking of type Bank |
# In the business book this is an asset: money the business has placed outside
# itself. Recategorising it as Equity:Owner Draw is the user's call, and the
# note is what prompts it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The id counter belongs to the book
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BusinessRule The id counter is recorded in the book, not worked out from it
Description Accounts and categories are identified by a number drawn from one
# shared counter -- see Accounts. The number the counter last handed
# out is a column of the book row, written every time one is given
# out, so the next id a book hands out after being opened again is
# one past the last it ever gave, not one past the largest it still
# holds.
Details The difference shows when something is deleted. A counter worked out
# from the chart would drop back when its highest account went, and the
# next account created would be handed a number something else still
# names -- a transaction in a file not yet imported, a backup opened
# later. A recorded counter cannot drop back.
#
# Nothing reads the number off a screen, so no scenario here states one.
# What is stated is where it lives and what it is not.
Examples: CounterStorage
| WhatIsAsked                               | Answer                                        |
| Where the last id handed out is kept      | The last_id_used column of the book row       |
| How the next id is found                  | One past it, and the column is written again   |
| What a delete does to it                  | Nothing; the column does not drop back        |
| What a book opened again counts from      | The column, not the largest id in the chart   |
| Whether a group takes an id of its own    | Yes; a filled-in parent is a node like any other |

Attributes CounterStorage
| Attribute   | Type   | Default |
| WhatIsAsked | String |         |
| Answer      | String |         |

Scenario Every node in the chart has an id, and the book records the last one
Description The only scenario in this specification that states an id. It can,
# because a new book is empty and the counter starts from nothing, so
# the numbers here are not something a reader has to look up -- they
# are the order the nodes were made in.
#
# A group is a node like any other, so it takes an id too. Nothing
# needs one yet, but they are there and they cost nothing, and a chart
# where some nodes have an identity and some do not is worse than one
# where they all do. Assets and Expenses are the two groups here, and
# each is numbered before the account under it.
Given a new book named Business
Given the chart of accounts is : ChartOfAccounts
| Path               | Type     | Placeholder |
| Assets             | Checking | true        |
| Assets:Checking    | Checking | false       |
| Expenses           | Expense  | true        |
| Expenses:Groceries | Expense  | false       |
When the book is saved and opened again
Then account ids are : AccountIdentity
| Path               | Id | Placeholder |
| Assets             | 1  | true        |
| Assets:Checking    | 2  | false       |
| Expenses           | 3  | true        |
| Expenses:Groceries | 4  | false       |
Then the book records : IdCounter Vertical
| LastIdUsed | 4 |
# Four nodes, four ids, and the book opens again counting from four. Deleting
# Expenses:Groceries would leave it at four, and the next account made would be
# five -- see the counter rule in Accounts.

Attributes AccountIdentity
| Attribute   | Type        | Default |
| Path        | AccountPath |         |
| Id          | Integer     | 0       |
| Placeholder | Boolean     | false   |

Attributes IdCounter
| Attribute  | Type    | Default |
| LastIdUsed | Integer | 0       |
